<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Hildebrandt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Hildebrandt was the Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a> of Mecklenburg who in 1933 became Reich governor of Mecklenburg-Schwerin and Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>, and from 1934 to 1945 of the merged Free State of Mecklenburg. An SS officer, he was tried after the war by an American military court for ordering the killing of captured Allied airmen, and he was executed at Landsberg in 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-hildebrandt.jpg" alt="Friedrich Hildebrandt" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Mohrmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HL_Damals_%E2%80%93_Hildebrandt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hildebrandt was born on 19 September 1898 at Kiekindemark, now part of Parchim, the son of a farm worker. He volunteered for the Imperial German Army in April 1916, was badly gassed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> in 1917 and won the Iron Cross. In 1919 he fought with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> von Brandis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a> and the Baltic until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> captured him at Riga. In 1920 he joined the security police in Halle and took part in putting down the Kapp-Putsch; tried for brutality against captured Spartakists, he was acquitted but dismissed. In 1924 he entered the Landtag of Mecklenburg-Schwerin for the German Völkisch Freedom Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He joined the refounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> in February 1925 and was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a> of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> on 27 March 1925. He founded the Niederdeutscher Beobachter in 1927 and entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> in September 1930. Suspended by Rudolf Hess in July 1930 for criticising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>'s alliance with industry, he was reinstated in January 1931 after declaring his loyalty. In 1933 he also became governor of the Free City of Lübeck, and he joined the SS that December, rising to SS-Obergruppenführer in 1942. He later commanded the Volkssturm in his Gau.</p><p>Hildebrandt took part in the murders of Aktion T4: in April 1941 he had the deaconess house at Lübtheen seized, and the mentally handicapped children living there were taken to Schwerin and later murdered. In the winter of 1941 to 1942 several thousand Soviet prisoners of war starved to death in Mecklenburg, and by the end of 1943 there were 152,148 foreign workers there, most held against their will as slave labourers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Arrested by the British after the war, he was handed to the Americans in 1946 for his part in orders to execute downed Allied airmen in 1944. The British and Americans refused repeated Soviet demands for his extradition over the euthanasia murders at Sachsenberg. At the Dachau trials in 1947 he and five others were sentenced to death for the killing of American airmen. Appeals for clemency failed, and he was hanged at Landsberg Prison on 5 November 1948, blaming Communists for his fate in his last statement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Friedrich Hildebrandt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Nazis the state was run by Reich governor <b>Friedrich Hildebrandt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-mecklenburg">Free State of Mecklenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1933 parliamentary rule ended and <b>Friedrich Hildebrandt</b> became Reich governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-mecklenburg-schwerin">Free State of Mecklenburg-Schwerin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kiekindemark, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Landsberg am Lech, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.4,11.78&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Kiekindemark on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4812 327 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Friedrich Hildebrandt"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5262 597h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5262 597h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.4,11.78&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
